  "content": "# Binary Search and `lower_bound` Explained\n\n## What is Binary Search?\n\nBinary search is an efficient algorithm for finding an item in a **sorted** list. Instead of checking every element one by one (which would take O(n) time), binary search repeatedly halves the search space:\n\n1. Look at the middle element.\n2. If it matches your target, you're done.\n3. If your target is smaller, search only the left half.\n4. If your target is larger, search only the right half.\n5. Repeat until you find the target or narrow the search space to nothing.\n\nThis gives us **O(log n)** time complexity \u2014 dramatically faster than linear search for large lists. For example, searching through 1 million items takes at most ~20 comparisons with binary search, versus up to 1 million with linear search.\n\n---\n\n## The `lower_bound` Function\n\n`lower_bound` returns the **index of the first element that is greater than or equal to** the target value. This is useful when:\n- You want to insert a new value into a sorted list while keeping it sorted.\n- You need to find where a target *would* be if it existed.\n",
